1. Chosen Development Model
State the chosen development model (e.g., Agile, Waterfall).
Justification: Briefly explain why this model was selected (e.g., flexibility, adaptability,
structure).

2. User Requirements
a. Stakeholders (WHO is involved)
• Identify key stakeholders (end-users, clients, developers, etc.)
• Briefly describe their roles and interests in the project.
b. User Stories (WHAT they need and WHY)
• Provide several detailed user stories
• Include the user type (e.g., admin, user)
• Clearly describe the requirement and its benefit
Example: As a customer, I want to book tickets online so that I can avoid waiting in line.

3. Functional Requirements (what the system must do)
a. Description
Write simple and clear descriptions of what the system must do.
Example: The system should allow users to register and log in.
b. Acceptance Criteria (When we know it WORKS correctly)
Create a checklist for each feature describing when it is considered complete.
Example: User Login
Acceptance Criteria:
-User enters valid email and password
-System verifies credentials

-User is redirected to dashboard after login
-Error message is shown for invalid login

4. Non-Functional Requirements (How well the system should work)
a. Description
Describe how well the system should perform (e.g., speed, usability, reliability).
Example: The system should load quickly.
b. Acceptance Criteria
Define measurable goals for each requirement (e.g., response time, usability standards).
Example: Speed
Acceptance Criteria:
-Pages load in less than 2 seconds.
-Booking process completes in under 5 seconds.

5. Application Specifications (How the system is built technically)
a. Architecture
Provide an overview of the system architecture, including components and interactions.
Example:
The system follows a three-tier architecture consisting of a frontend (user interface),
backend (business logic), and database. The frontend sends requests to the backend, which
processes data and communicates with the database.
b. Database Model
Describe the database structure: tables, relationships, and constraints.
Example:
The database includes tables such as Users, Movies, and Bookings. Each booking is linked to
a user and a movie. The email field in the Users table is unique, and all bookings must
reference a valid user.
c. Technologies Used
List technologies, and languages used, including reasons for selection.
Example: The system is developed using Python for backend development due to its
simplicity and scalability. The frontend uses HTML, CSS, and JavaScript for a responsive
interface. MySQL is used as the database for efficient data management.
d. User Interface Design
Provide wireframes, mockups, or descriptions of the user interface.

Example: The interface includes a login page, a movie browsing page, and a booking page.
The design is simple and user-friendly, with clear navigation and buttons for key actions
such as booking and payment.
e. Security Measures
Describe security measures such as authentication, encryption, and data protection.
Example: The system uses user authentication with secure login credentials. Passwords are
encrypted before storage. Role-based access control ensures that only admins can manage
system data.
